 of time and energy  required to get someone to to flip their  votes versus simply because we don't  have mandatory voting finding people who  are unlikely to vote but already agree  with you my instinct is the time and  energy is way better spent just getting  the vote out with people you don't have  to convince rather than arguing with  people and I think you know imagining me  arguing with a trumpet in in North  Dakota trying to get them to change the  rope versus just finding 10 Democratic  votes it seems instinctually accurate  but but is it do you have a feeling  about that yeah we for sure no that's  true like if you are if you're in a  situation where it's a Democratic versus  Republican election and you have the  choice of like trying to persuade a  bunch of people on the other side or  turnout the people you  we actually know a lot more from great  experiments about how to increase  turnout without trying to persuade  anyone by giving people reminders by  taking people to the polls by like  making voting easier for them what I  would say is though on a lot of the  issues that people care about there's  much more persuasion to be done than we  might think and that's because you know  some people say like they live in a  bubble a lot of people say like oh I  live in a liberal bubble or a  conservative bubble there's no one I  need to convince and what they mean by  that is like everyone on the  presidential election around them is  basically going to vote the same way but  when it comes to actual issues and those  issues are more manifest in local and  state elections people are not such an  above all people have really different  perspectives on this you know some  people don't want to have a 5 cent  increase in the gas tax some people  don't really want to solve a massive  transportation problem in a region and  so there actually is a lot more  persuasion to be done than you might  think it's just not going to be in the  terms of like you know a Fox News and  MSNBC viewer fighting with each other  that's not going to be ever very  productive absolutely absolutely agree  with you there we've been speaking with  Eitan Hirsch who teaches a political  science at Tufts University is also  author of the book politics is for power  which we are linking to 8 on so great  having you on and I really appreciate  your time  thanks for having me
 

